{
  "_comment": [
    "A1 Trading's per-country economic heatmap cards, transcribed from their own screen.",
    "EVIDENCE, not configuration — nothing reads this at runtime. `npm run cards` scores",
    "our per-currency heatmap against it; `fixtures/a1-board.json` is its sibling and holds",
    "their Top Setups board instead.",
    "",
    "WHY A SECOND FIXTURE, AND NOT MORE ROWS IN THE FIRST ONE.",
    "The board publishes a SCORE. These cards publish the four numbers BEHIND a score —",
    "Actual, Forecast, Previous, Surprise — which is exactly what `npm run legs` prints for",
    "our side. Comparing the two is a direct reading of both models. Comparing board totals",
    "is an inference from the sum of eighteen of them, and this repo has repeatedly drawn",
    "the wrong conclusion from that inference.",
    "",
    "STRUCTURAL EVIDENCE vs VALUE EVIDENCE, and only the first survives an unknown date.",
    "  STRUCTURAL — which rows a card carries, which series variant each names (MoM / YoY /",
    "    QoQ), and whether the Forecast column is BLANK. These are properties of their",
    "    model. They are attributable regardless of when the frame was taken.",
    "  VALUE — actual, forecast, previous, surprise, the impacts, the percentage. These are",
    "    properties of a MOMENT. The capture below has no recoverable date, so nothing may",
    "    be concluded from a value disagreement. `valuesUsable` says so in machine-readable",
    "    form; `npm run cards` prints the value block under its own warning.",
    "",
    "`forecast: null` MEANS THE COLUMN WAS BLANK ON THE CARD. It never means illegible —",
    "an unreadable cell gets an `illegible` list and is excluded from the basis check. The",
    "distinction is the whole value of this file for `compareByCurrency`: a blank Forecast",
    "is evidence they scored the row against the PREVIOUS print. Conflating the two would",
    "manufacture config from a bad screenshot, which `config/setups.config.ts` warns about",
    "at length under `compareByCurrency` — and a blank on ONE card is still a snapshot, not",
    "a property of the series. Set config from it only where our own feed also carries no",
    "consensus.",
    "",
    "`slotKey` IS OURS, NOT THEIRS. It is the mapping assertion, and a null is itself a",
    "finding: a row on their card we have no slot for.",
    "",
    "TWO SELF-CHECKS, both asserted in `lib/scoring/heatmap.test.ts`:",
    "  1. per row, surprise === actual - (forecast ?? previous)",
    "  2. per card, bullishShare(currencyImpacts) === impactPct.currency",
    "The second is the stronger one and it is how the neutral-exclusion rule was settled:",
    "counting neutrals in the denominator reproduces NONE of the nine percentages below.",
    "",
    "Captures are newest first, append-only. An old one costs a few hundred bytes and",
    "re-taking one is impossible."
  ],
  "captures": [
    {
      "capturedUtc": null,
      "provenance": "screenshots supplied by the user; date not recoverable",
      "valuesUsable": false,
      "note": [
        "ALL NINE CARDS AT ONCE — US, EU, UK, JP, CA, AU, NZ, CH, CN. The first time their",
        "per-country row sets have existed in structured form.",
        "",
        "The dates INSIDE the rows run Jul 2024 to Jan 2026, so the frames are from roughly",
        "late January 2026 — but that is inferred from release dates rather than read off the",
        "stream, and one row (JP Services PMI, Jul 24 24) is over a year stale even relative",
        "to that. Not enough to score values against. `capturedUtc` stays null.",
        "",
        "THAT STALE ROW IS NOT THE ONLY ONE, AND THE QUESTION IS NOW CLOSED.",
        "",
        "Four rows here sit past our own freshness windows and A1 score every one of them:",
        "  JP Services PMIs  Jul 24, 24  ~547 days  vs our sPMI window   60  scored +1",
        "  AU Retail Sales   Jul 30, 25  ~176 days  vs our retail window 75  scored +1",
        "  NZ CPI YoY        Oct 19, 25   ~95 days  vs our CPI window    60  scored  0",
        "  NZ Unemployment   Nov  4, 25   ~79 days  vs our unemp window  60  scored  0",
        "",
        "Read together that looks like no freshness cutoff at all, which would be a real",
        "difference in how the two engines treat an old print. IT CHANGES NOTHING TODAY, and",
        "that was measured rather than assumed: every `maxAgeDays` was widened to ten years and",
        "the 2026-08-24 board rebuilt, and ZERO cells moved across all 51 rows. Every print our",
        "feed carries is already inside its window, so this divergence is LATENT — it can only",
        "bite on a day when a series lapses, and we have no capture of such a day.",
        "",
        "So the windows stay as they are. Widening every freshness rule to fix nothing is pure",
        "downside, and `resolveSeries` ranks freshness ABOVE scoreability — a wider window does",
        "not merely admit more prints, it can change which series a column picks. Re-run the",
        "measurement before touching one.",
        "",
        "WHAT THIS CAPTURE ALREADY SETTLED:",
        "  - Nine independent confirmations of bullish / (bullish + bearish), neutrals",
        "    excluded. See `bullishShare`.",
        "  - Five confirmations of the forecast -> previous fallback, from cards where the",
        "    Forecast column is blank and Surprise is exactly actual - previous: CH PPI",
        "    (-1.8 vs -1.6 = -0.2), CH Unemployment (3.1 vs 2.9 = 0.2), CA Retail Sales",
        "    (1.2 vs -0.7 = 1.9), UK PPI (3.4 vs 3.4 = 0.0), NZ mPMI (56.1 vs 51.4 = 4.7).",
        "  - The US card carries WAGE GROWTH YoY and it counts toward the 58.33%. We had no",
        "    slot for it; `wage-growth` was added from this evidence, US-only, heatmap-only.",
        "  - NO CARD CARRIES A CONSUMER CONFIDENCE ROW, and none carries an Interest Rates",
        "    row, while their Top Setups has a column for both. That asymmetry is why",
        "    `matrixOnly` exists.",
        "  - The CA card carries Employment Change, which we had scoped to the euro area",
        "    alone.",
        "",
        "THE EU EMPLOYMENT CHANGE CONFLICT, recorded rather than resolved. This EU card has",
        "SEVEN rows and reads 57.14% (4 of 7). An older EU card, cited in",
        "`lib/scoring/heatmap.ts` and `config/setups.config.ts`, had EIGHT rows reading 50%",
        "(2 bull, 2 bear, 4 neutral) and carried Employment Change. Both fit the percentage",
        "rule, so neither reading is wrong — their card set moves. Do NOT delete the EUR",
        "employment-change matcher on the strength of this capture alone; `npm run cards`",
        "reports it as our one remaining ours-only row and that is the honest state.",
        "",
        "COSMETIC, so nobody re-derives them:",
        "  - The Surprise cell's COLOUR tracks the STOCKS impact, not the currency impact.",
        "    Every inflation row on every card shows a red Surprise beside a Bullish currency",
        "    reading. It carries no information the impact columns do not.",
        "",
        "CN IS STORED AND SCORES NOTHING. We have no CNH, CHINA50 has no counterpart in our",
        "symbol set, and `TRADINGVIEW.countries` omits CN deliberately. It is here because it",
        "is a ninth independent confirmation of the impact-percentage rule."
